  <entry>
    <title>suda.vim for sudo access to files</title>
    <id>https://pype.dev/suda-vim-for-sudo-access-to-files/</id>
    <updated>2022-12-21T09:45:34Z</updated>
    <published>2022-12-21T09:45:34Z</published>
    <link href="https://pype.dev/suda-vim-for-sudo-access-to-files/" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link>
    <summary type="text">I regularly need to edit system config files - take /etc/sanoid/sanoid.conf as an example... I&#39;ll want to play with something but if I don&#39;t start Neovim as...</summary>
    <content type="html">&lt;p&gt;I regularly need to edit system config files - take /etc/sanoid/sanoid.conf as&#xA;an example&amp;hellip; I&amp;rsquo;ll want to play with something but if I don&amp;rsquo;t start Neovim as&#xA;root then I get in trouble making edits I can&amp;rsquo;t save! So&#xA;&lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/lambdalisue/suda.vim&#34;&gt;suda.vim&lt;/a&gt; gives me&#xA;&lt;code&gt;:SudaWrite&lt;/code&gt; which let&amp;rsquo;s me write that buffer with sudo privileges even though&#xA;I&amp;rsquo;m Neovim is running with my login user!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;</content>
    <author>
      <name>Nic Payne</name>
      <uri>https://pype.dev</uri>
    </author>
  </entry>
